      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Introduction</h3><p>In previous classes, we learned how to walk around a circle and, as a result, how we are tangent to it (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). Then we learned why we stay on a planet: according to Newton's model, there is a force pointing toward the Earth's center that keeps us on the planet (<htcite2>).</p><p>Now, let’s reflect:</p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: What do we need to walk on a planet?</b><br /><br />To find our position on a circle, we needed two coordinates: <b>r</b> × cos(<b>θ</b>) and <b>r</b> × sin(<b>θ</b>) (<htcite3>). Now think: if we want to walk on a planet, what else do we need to know?</div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1cc3be4f-110f-40eb-b281-d01378aae23f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking on the Circle",
