      {
         "id": "SECTION_coordinates",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Coordinates</h3><p>Now we are going to study coordinates. A coordinate is a number that tells us where something is. Think of a cinema ticket: one number gives the row and another gives the seat. We can use one, two or three coordinates, depending on the space we want to describe: one for a straight line, two for a flat map, three for a sphere. Let’s begin with the simplest case and add one coordinate at a time.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_linear_coordinates",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h4>Linear coordinates</h4><p>In a linear dimension there is only one direction. To find our position, we measure from a starting point, called the origin, to the place where we are. It is like using a ruler: if we walk 5 meters from the origin along a straight line, the single number 5 m tells us exactly where we are. One coordinate is enough because there is only one direction to move along (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "87f5f133-067e-439f-ab1d-eaf45e304079",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "When size matters",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 220\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:760px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"A horizontal axis starting at the origin, with a red vector of 5 meters pointing to the right\"><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"644.0\" y2=\"140.0\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"644.0,147.0 660,140 644.0,133.0\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">0</text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">1</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">2</text><line x1=\"260\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">3</text><line x1=\"320\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">4</text><line x1=\"380\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"148\" stroke=\"#777777\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"380\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#666666\">5</text><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"140\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"364.0\" y2=\"140.0\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"7\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"364.0,148.0 380,140 364.0,132.0\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" font-weight=\"bold\">d = 5 m</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: A vector in a linear dimension. It starts at the origin and shows a value of 5 m in a single direction.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_two_coordinates",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h4>Two coordinates</h4><p>When we can move in two directions, one coordinate is not enough. We need two independent coordinates, one for each direction. We measure both from the same origin, following two separate directions that we usually call X and Y. For example, to locate a point on a flat surface, we say how far to go along X and how far to go along Y (<htcite0>).</p><p>A circle's radius follows the rules we have already studied. If we draw a radius r at an angle θ, its horizontal part is r × cos(θ) and its vertical part is r × sin(θ). These are the same two coordinates written in two ways: linear coordinates (x, y) or radial coordinates (r, θ) (<htcite1>).</p><p>Because X and Y are perpendicular, they form the legs of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the radius r. Here the <b>Babylonian Diagonal Triple</b>, also known as the <b>Pythagorean Theorem</b>, helps us: the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the legs, so r<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> (<htcite2>). For example, if x = 4 m and y = 3 m, then 4<sup>2</sup> + 3<sup>2</sup> = 16 + 9 = 25, which gives r = √25 = 5 m.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03bb4b8e-6187-4572-8f00-ea3892df51a4",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Comparing in more than one direction",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "931557c0-ca2b-4b50-b034-1ed53752c58e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Where are we on a disk?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f5cb70e-d246-49b2-ae2a-9841ffc1fb14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Practicing with the Theorem",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 400\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:760px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"Two perpendicular axes X and Y. A red vector from the origin reaches a point at four meters along X and three meters along Y; the same vector has radius five meters and angle thirty-seven degrees\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">Linear: (x, y) = (4 m, 3 m)</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">Radial: (r, θ) = (5 m, 37°)</text><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"340\" x2=\"684.0\" y2=\"340.0\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"684.0,347.0 700,340 684.0,333.0\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"365\" x2=\"80.0\" y2=\"46.0\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"87.0,46.0 80,30 73.0,46.0\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"420\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"340\" stroke=\"#999999\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><line x1=\"420\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#999999\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"340\" x2=\"406.0\" y2=\"340.0\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"406.0,347.0 420,340 406.0,333.0\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"340\" x2=\"80.0\" y2=\"99.0\" stroke=\"#27ae60\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"87.0,99.0 80,85 73.0,99.0\" fill=\"#27ae60\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"340\" x2=\"408.8\" y2=\"93.4\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"412.4,98.2 420,85 405.2,88.6\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><path d=\"M 150.0 340.0 A 70 70 0 0 0 136.0 298.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"158\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"22\" font-style=\"italic\" fill=\"#333333\">θ</text><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"340\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><text x=\"66\" y=\"358\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#333333\">O</text><text x=\"708\" y=\"347\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#333333\">X</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333333\">Y</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"366\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\">x = 4 m</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#27ae60\">y = 3 m</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#c0392b\">r = 5 m</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#333333\">P (4, 3)</text><circle cx=\"420\" cy=\"85\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 4</b>: The same vector written in two ways. In linear coordinates it has x = 4 m and y = 3 m; in radial coordinates it has r = 5 m and θ ≈ 37°.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_three_coordinates",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h4>The third coordinate</h4><p>To describe a position outside a flat surface, two coordinates are still not enough. Imagine a balloon: a point on its surface is not on the flat map below it. We need a third coordinate that tells us how far the point is from the flat plane — how high it lifts off the map. This gives us spherical coordinates: r is the distance from the origin, θ gives the direction on the flat plane, and φ gives the angle measured from the vertical axis.</p><p>The same Babylonian Diagonal Triple (Pythagorean Theorem) can be expanded to three dimensions. When the third coordinate z is perpendicular to X and Y, the square of the spherical radius is the sum of the squares of the three coordinates: r<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> + z<sup>2</sup>. In our example, r<sup>2</sup> = 4<sup>2</sup> + 3<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> = 16 + 9 + 25 = 50, so r = √50 ≈ 7.1 m.</p><p>Notice the change: in the flat plane the radius was 5 m, but in three dimensions it is 7.1 m. <span style=\"background-color:#fff3cd; padding:0 4px; border-radius:3px;\"><b>This change happens because we added a new dimension: the height z = 5 m pushes the point farther from the origin.</b></span></p><p>With these three coordinates we can locate any point on a sphere — for example, any place on the surface of the Earth (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fd003897-89a6-4d01-ae79-da9e564d9583",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
