         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The arrows on top of the Earth in <b>Figure 1</b> are the same arrows we see in <b>Figure 5</b>. In both figures the spherical coordinate origin is the center of the sphere, and the group <b>r</b>, <b>θ</b> and <b>φ</b> is attached to a point on its surface. These spherical directions are local: they change from point to point. The radial direction <b>r</b> follows the line <b>OP</b> — for example, at <b>P</b> = (4, 3, 5), <b>r</b> points from the origin <b>O</b> to <b>P</b> — and the angles <b>θ</b> and <b>φ</b> are measured at the origin. The Cartesian axes <b>X</b>, <b>Y</b> and <b>Z</b>, by contrast, are fixed directions of the coordinate system: they do not move with the point. With the third coordinate, the spherical coordinates can describe any point of the sphere.</p><p><b>A small note</b>: the equation r<sup>2</sup> = 25 has two real solutions, 5 and -5, but the square root √25 is 5. We use the nonnegative solution because r is a distance. In short: one coordinate for a line, two for a flat surface, three for a sphere.</p>",
